<title>USA: U.S. Sept construction spending rose 1.9 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Sept construction spending rose 1.9 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-11-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Commerce Department seasonally adjusted construction spending, with comparisons.</p>
<p>    PERCENT CHANGES: Sept     Aug--Rev from  Sept96/95</p>
<p> Total Spending	 1.9     0.7     0.9	  4.2</p>
<p>ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:  Sept'95</p>
<p> Total Spending     573.4   562.7   562.1	550.5</p>
<p> Private Spending   426.4   424.6   423.4	411.3</p>
<p> -Residential	 244.1   244.3   244.0	237.7</p>
<p> --New Housing	174.8   175.8   175.6	164.3</p>
<p> ---Single-Family   155.9   157.3   157.1	146.0</p>
<p> ---Multi-Family     19.0    18.4    18.5	 18.3</p>
<p>ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:</p>
<p> -			 Sept     Aug--Rev from  Sept'95</p>
<p> -Nonresidential    142.3   140.3   139.5	134.8</p>
<p> --Industrial	  29.1    27.1    27.8	 32.4</p>
<p> --Office		26.2    25.8    25.6	 25.0</p>
<p> Public Spending    147.0   138.1   138.7	139.1</p>
<p>    PERCENT CHANGES: Sept     Aug--Rev from  Sept96/95</p>
<p> Private Spending     0.4     1.4     1.3	  3.7</p>
<p> Public Spending	6.4    -1.4    -0.2	  5.7</p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected a 0.1 percent drop in September construction spending.  </p>
<p>Construction spending in 1992 dollars, which discounts the effects of inflation:</p>
<p>  Percent Changes -- Sept     Aug--Rev from</p>
<p>  Total Spending	1.7     0.5     0.7</p>
<p>Annual Rates, Billions of U.S. Dollars --</p>
<p>  Total Spending    496.3   488.1   488.1</p>
<p>The department said the 1.9 percent increase in September construction spending to a record $573.4 billion was the biggest monthly gain since a 2.3 percent gain in March.</p>
<p>The 6.4 percent jump in September public construction spending was the biggest in more than 5-1/2 years since a 9.5 percent jump in February 1991, the department said.</p>
<p>The department revised overall July construction spending to a 0.8 percent drop from a 1.1 percent decrease.</p>
